The constraint check for 6 Larch Grove shows it is not in a conservation area, is not a listed building, and has no tree preservation order. That helps, because those are the situations where the rules are tighter.

The council guidance on outbuildings says that garden rooms and sheds usually don't need an application if they meet all of these conditions:

- No more than 2.5 metres high
- Behind the front wall of the house
- Covering less than half the garden

Whether your garden room meets these depends on its height, where it sits relative to the house's front wall, and how much of the garden it covers. I can't give a formal planning opinion, so if any of these is uncertain, or if the building is larger than the conditions allow, you should apply for a formal decision. Your site plan would be part of that application, so keep a copy ready.

If you can tell me the planned height, the position, and the approximate footprint, I can help you check it against these points. You can also contact the council planning desk to confirm before you build.
